  "narrative": "No new independent validation estimates were released and there were no response deadlines pending; the validation budget is exhausted. The scientific synthesis therefore rests on pre-specified discovery contrasts already performed in this supplied dataset, and it remains limited to observational associations within this dataset. I do not infer causality, biological mechanism, clinical relevance, or external generalizability from any claim.\\n\\nIn all reported estimates, the values are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. Thus a positive signed estimate supports the claimed direction. For direction -1 claims, support means the raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast is more negative, i.e. stronger dependency. For direction +1 claims, support means the raw contrast is less negative or more positive, i.e. weaker dependency. I used the outcome-specific absolute threshold of 0.15 composite dependency-score units, and I treated an association as robust when the signed point estimate and lower confidence bound both cleared the threshold, except where independent validation had already replicated the same contrast.\\n\\nThe strongest supported association is SMARCA4 loss with stronger dependency. The overall contrast is approximately +0.83 in discovery and was independently replicated at approximately +0.80 under validation. This association remains robust across multiple biological and technical strata, including lineage subtype, age, CRISPR library, screen batch, mutation-burden bands, Cas9-activity bands, screen-quality bands, and slow-growth contexts. The direct fast-growth and middle-growth rate contexts remain untested. The second strongest supported association is MSI-high with stronger dependency. The signed overall effect is approximately +0.79 in discovery and approximately +0.81 in validation, and the association is accepted in many strata, including both slow and fast proliferation contexts. The low mutation-burden context is not evaluable because no MSI-high cases satisfy the low mutation-burden cutoff, so the next most informative TMB question is whether MSI-high remains strongly associated in the middle mutation-burden band. KEAP1 loss with weaker dependency is also very robust, with discovery and validation signed estimates near +0.80, and it persists across most strata examined. TP53 loss with weaker dependency is robust as well, with overall discovery and validation signed estimates near +0.42 and +0.38, respectively, and with persistence across many strata, though growth-rate contexts remain incomplete.\\n\\nPTEN loss is a smaller but recurrent stronger-dependency association. Its overall signed discovery estimate is approximately +0.23 and validation estimate approximately +0.34. It is accepted cautiously in several clean or replication-supporting contexts, including triple-intact predictor sets, TP53-intact settings, KEAP1-positive settings, and low mutation burden. However, many PTEN context-specific estimates are not sufficiently precise to support robust effect modification. Fast growth-rate PTEN R173 has signed estimate +0.331 with lower bound 0.144, so it is borderline but does not clear the 0.15 threshold and remains unresolved rather than accepted. PTEN at mutation_burden >=5 is positive but imprecise and remains deferred. PTEN middle growth-rate and middle mutation-burden contexts are the most important next PTEN analyses. PTEN-by-growth-rate interactions were not supported.\\n\\nNegative or unsupported comparisons include KRAS mutation, MTAP loss, ARID1A loss, STK11 loss, CDKN2A loss, BRCA2 loss, EGFR amplification, ERBB2 amplification, APC mutation, SMAD4 loss, NF1 loss, BRAF V600E, MYC amplification overall and under high MYC expression, and the constructed research markers D/E/F. Global technical or culture comparisons generally did not clear the threshold or were not consistently supported, including media serum percentage, screen batch, CRISPR library category, RNA/DNA availability, and sex. Formal interaction analyses were mostly null or inconsistent. Rejected or unsupported interaction claims include SMARCA4-by-TP53, KEAP1-by-SMARCA4, PTEN-by-SMARCA4, PTEN-by-growth-rate interactions, and several research-signature or context-stratified interactions. A KEAP1-by-TP53 interaction signal in adenocarcinoma did not survive independent validation. I have not treated any currently available interaction as validated.\\n\\nThe main unresolved issues are proliferation context, PTEN stability across mutation burden, MSI-high independence from mutation burden, and direct research-signature associations. For research signatures, the attempted direct one-unit contrasts failed because the controller interpreted exact continuous values as empty comparison cells. Closing those claims should be read as an implementation limitation, not as evidence that signatures are unrelated. If a valid grouped or tertile representation of continuous signatures becomes available, direct association tests remain scientifically useful, but they are not currently interpretable as null results.\\n\\nFor the next analysis, I propose MSI-high in the middle mutation-burden band, plus SMARCA4, KEAP1, TP53, and MSI-high in the middle growth-rate band. These complement the already-registered active fast-growth contexts H196, H198, and H200, slow-growth TP53 and MSI contexts H201 and H203, PTEN middle growth-rate H208, and PTEN middle mutation-burden H209. If the direct fast and middle growth-rate main effects are robust, growth-rate interaction claims become informative; if they are not robust, many growth-rate interaction claims should be closed. The immediate priority is to determine whether the accepted main predictors attenuate in proliferative contexts and whether the smaller PTEN association is stable across the middle growth-rate and mutation-burden bands. All conclusions remain within-dataset observational associations only.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted within-dataset associations: SMARCA4 loss with stronger dependency, MSI-high with stronger dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker dependency, TP53 loss with weaker dependency, and PTEN loss with a smaller recurrent stronger-dependency association. SMARCA4, MSI-high, KEAP1, and TP53 have broad cross-stratum support and independent validation for main overall contrasts. PTEN support is real but smaller and context-sensitive.\\n\\nGrowth-rate status: slow growth-rate contexts accepted for SMARCA4 H197, KEAP1 H199, and MSI-high H202; PTEN slow growth-rate is rejected; PTEN fast growth-rate is borderline unresolved because lower bound 0.144 is just below threshold. Active direct fast growth-rate contexts are H196 SMARCA4, H198 KEAP1, and H200 TP53. Active slow growth-rate contexts are H201 TP53 and H203 MSI-high. Middle growth-rate contexts newly registered for SMARCA4, KEAP1, TP53, and MSI-high; PTEN middle growth-rate already registered as H208.\\n\\nMutation-burden status: MSI-high is strong overall and in high TMB, but low TMB is not evaluable because no MSI-high cells meet low mutation-burden cutoff. New proposal tests MSI-high in middle mutation-burden band. PTEN middle mutation-burden H209 is active; PTEN high mutation burden H205 is positive but imprecise and deferred; PTEN low mutation burden remains accepted in replicated context.\\n\\nInteractions: no validated effect modification is currently supported. Growth-rate interactions H206/H207 are deferred until direct growth-rate main effects are assessed. Screen-doubling interactions H187/H188 are deferred. PTEN-by-growth interactions were rejected. KEAP1-by-TP53 adenocarcinoma interaction failed validation.\\n\\nResearch signatures: direct one-unit continuous contrast claims H191-H193 closed as unresolved because exact continuous exposure values produced empty cells. This is not evidence of no association. Direct signature main effects would require valid grouped/tertile/one-SD encoding not available in current exact-value coding.\\n\\nNext analyze priorities: direct fast growth-rate claims H196, H198, H200; direct slow growth-rate claims H201, H203; PTEN middle growth-rate H208; PTEN middle mutation-burden H209; new MSI-high middle mutation-burden; new middle growth-rate main-effect claims for SMARCA4, KEAP1, TP53, MSI-high if analysis slots permit. Defer low-priority technical library contexts and PTEN-by-TP53 contexts unless direct main-effect results make them essential.\\n\\nAll claims are observational within the supplied dataset. They do not establish causality, biological pathway mechanism, clinical relevance, or generalizability."
